  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nModel run to run agreement is still poor overall, so the details \neven in the short term are not as certain as would be preferred. \nHowever, the basic pattern is not much in doubt with an upper low \nmoving out of the mid Mississippi Valley and into the Ohio River \nValley/southern Great Lakes region through Sunday. A cold front will \napproach us from the west and eventually move into/through our area \nSaturday night and/or Sunday. The details of how strong and the \nexact track of both the low pressure system and how quickly the \nfront will move into and through our area have been handled very \ninconsistently by the models.  However, there is high confidence \nthat showers and thunderstorms will move into our area later tonight \nand this morning as moisture surges in with the increasing southerly \nflow and divergence aloft ahead of the upper trough.  This \nthunderstorm activity is expected to be sub-severe, as CAPE will be \nmodest and the convection likely elevated at first.  \n\nClouds and convection during the day Saturday will impact how much \ninstability develops, but it currently looks like at least moderate \nMLCAPE of 1000 to 1500 J/kg will develop over western areas during \nthe afternoon.  By late today/this evening the front will be moving \ninto middle TN, and the CAMS generally show additional convection \nfiring ahead of the front and moving east toward our area. Models \nhave around 20 to 30 kts of effective shear which given at least \nmoderate instability would support organization of the convection \nand the potential for some strong to severe storms with damaging \nwinds the primary threat. The best low level shear looks to stay to \nour north and northwest, but there is sufficient low level shear and \nclockwise curvature of the hodograph that a tornado cannot be ruled \nout especially if the CAPE and shear potential is maximized.  The \nsevere potential should be highest for our Plateau and southern \nvalley counties where the convection will arrive first and surface \nCAPE will have less time to decrease from loss of solar heating. The \nlow level jet will be out of the south and southeast across the \nmountains Saturday and Saturday night, with downslope flow into the \nvalley. In fact, we will likely see gusty winds in the usual higher \nmountain elevation and foothill areas with some mountain wave \nenhancement of the winds, although it generally should stay sub-wind \nadvisory level. Given this downslope flow as well as the later \narrival of the convection in our central and northern/eastern areas, \nit is not surprising that the CAMS quickly weaken the convection as \nit progresses further east across our area. \n  \n\nIn addition to the severe threat, given the abundant moisture with \nPWAT values near or exceeding 2 inches for Saturday into Saturday \nnight very heavy rainfall and localized flooding will also be \npossible, especially across the Plateau and southern portions of our \narea.   \n\nDepending on how quickly the front clears our area, a marginal \nsevere storm threat may continue into Sunday over our northeast \ncounties ahead of the boundary where significant effective shear and \ninstability will be lingering ahead of the front. Behind the front, \nany showers and storms should be more isolated to scattered and \nweaker Sunday. \n\nThe later periods continue to feature significant chances for \nshowers and storms each day Monday through Friday but with the \nhighest chances early in the week in response to a meandering weak \nupper low that most models leave behind over the region. Models \nstill disagree on placement and movement of the upper low, and some, \nwhile in the minority, would suggest a quicker return to drier and \nwarmer conditions. We will have to see as we get closer.",
